Each binding exposes the same application operations over the C ABI. Native
bindings load the Host and the native loop, then add transport archives as
needed. The browser package uses WebAssembly and drives the Host itself.
[Choose an API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares
these bindings with the Zig package, C ABI, CLI, and browser package.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the capability model behind them.

[All four bindings wrap the same C ABI, so the session operations and transport choices stay aligned across languages.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-bindings.svg'))

# One contract, four idioms

A `Session` gives you the verbs in the
[bindings contract](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/README.md).
A `Host` is the multi-session product behind introductions
([modules]($link.page('modules'))). A `Ref` is a capability in hand; pass it as
an argument and authority moves.

| Binding | Entry point | Async idiom | Actor idiom |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')) | `Runtime`, `Session`, typed enums | tokio (opt-in `async`) | `spawn` over closures |
| [TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')) | `Runtime`, `Session` class | native promises | `spawn` over callbacks |
| [Python]($link.page('bindings/python')) | `Runtime`, `Session`, native values | `asyncio` | `spawn` over callables |
| [Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')) | `Runtime`, `session`, event structs | green threads + ports | Goblins-faithful |

`Runtime.open(...)` installs transport authority once. `runtime.connect(locator)`
returns an established session (handshake complete); per-operation options stay
on that call.

The TypeScript binding also runs in the browser, where `@glowcap/web` compiles
the same `Session` API to WebAssembly. The browser side always dials
rather than listens, and [In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser')) is
the browser setup guide.

Stay at the level you need: synchronous verbs, the language's own async sockets,
or the Goblins-shaped actor layer on top with near-call and eventual-send
([modules]($link.page('modules'))).

# Use a binding from a checkout

The packages and archives live in a glowcap checkout. Pin a commit, run
`zig build`, and use the binding in that tree. Bindings find
`zig-out/lib` by default; set `GLOWCAP_LIB_DIR` if the archives live elsewhere.

The [binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/README.md)
has the path for each language. Compose
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')),
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')),
[Noise]($link.page('transports/noise')), or
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc'))
from their archives. The TCP archive also carries testing `tcp`.
